The real cutoff is weather and mood, not the date on your phone. If you are still in sandals and the evenings have not cooled off, a bright shade will not look out of place. What tends to happen instead is a gradual muting. A hot pink shifts toward dusty rose. A tropical orange leans toward terracotta. You keep the warmth and the saturation drops slightly, which reads as intentional rather than dated.

Where this breaks down is late in the month, once jackets are back and the light has changed. A neon shade against grey skies and knitwear starts to look like an oversight rather than a choice. Early September gives you permission. Late September asks for a shift. Watching your own climate matters more than any generic seasonal rule here, especially if you live somewhere the heat holds on well past Labor Day.

